example:
csrWrapper.api.user.findOrders({ 
userId: '69aa174aba9d8bf27771b282', 
});

param
- lastOrderId: (optional) Pass lastOrderId to get the next N orders.

response
- schedule: This is the next payment schedule for this order. You can change the next schedule time with [Update Schedule DueTimestamp].

csrWrapper.api.user.getOrder({ 
userId: '69aa174aba9d8bf27771b282',
orderId: '69aa174aba9d8bf27771b299',
lastPaymentId: '69ab30d430ad87f2692196a7'
});

You can retrieve the same order results as [Find User Orders] by specifying one.

params
- lastPaymentId: (optional) If you need to change the status of a specific order in the entire order list and re-fetch only that order to update its status, you can pass the last _id of the commercePayments fetched by the current order to lastPaymentId to retrieve the commercePayments up to that id.

csrWrapper.api.user.findOrderPayments({ 
orderId: '69aa174aba9d8bf27771b299', 
lastPaymentId: '69aa174aba9d8bf27771b2a2'
});

The response from [Find User Orders] contains an array with a commercePayments field for each order. Only the first N items are returned. To retrieve additional commercePayments , pass the orderId and lastPaymentId .

csrWrapper.api.user.findOrderHistories({ 
orderId: '69aa174aba9d8bf27771b299',
lastRevisionId: '69aa1766ba9d8bf27771b33e',
});

The response from [Find User Orders] contains an orderHistories array field for each order. Initially, only N items are returned from this array. To receive the next N items, you must pass orderId and lastRevisionId . lastRevisionId is the _id of each orderHistories .

csrWrapper.api.user.cancelUncancelOrder({ 
orderId: '69aa174aba9d8bf27771b299',
flag: true,
});

params
- flag: true (cancels the order), false (reactivates the canceled order)

